From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: GKI KernelSU SUSFS <root>
Date: Mon Jan  1 00:00:00 2026 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fuse: disable MTK blocktag vendor hook entirely

MTK's blocktag vendor hook (BTMTR) emits block_io_start and
block_io_get_result trace calls while holding fiq->lock. When the
vendor's io-called hook needs a synchronous FUSE request (e.g. after
statfs()/getattr()), this deadlocks: the tracing triggers a sync
request which competes for fiq->lock (or hangs the requesting thread),
manifesting as a hang, watchdog reset, or SoftLockup right after boot
once userspace performs statfs() checks.

Additionally, on MT6893/lyriq the hook itself crashes: under container
FUSE activity (fuse_file_put -> fuse_simple_background -> flush_bg_queue
-> queue_request_and_unlock) btag_fuse_queue_request_and_unlock performs
a write to read-only memory (level 3 permission fault, ESR 0x9600004f),
causing "Kernel panic - not syncing: Oops: Fatal exception" and a
random reboot. The hook is kept for neither sync nor async requests:
FUSE is not block I/O, so losing blocktag QoS tagging here is harmless.

Signed-off-by: GKI KernelSU SUSFS <root>
diff --git a/fs/fuse/dev.c b/fs/fuse/dev.c
index d0bf4bc..ebfc006 100644
--- a/fs/fuse/dev.c
+++ b/fs/fuse/dev.c
@@ -236,5 +236,14 @@ __releases(fiq->lock)
 		fuse_len_args(req->args->in_numargs,
 			      (struct fuse_arg *) req->args->in_args);
 	list_add_tail(&req->list, &fiq->pending);
-	trace_android_vh_queue_request_and_unlock(&fiq->waitq, sync);
+	/*
+	 * MTK blocktag's block_io_start/block_io_get_result trace calls
+	 * are not safe to run while holding fiq->lock (deadlock/hang after
+	 * statfs()), and on some devices the hook writes to read-only
+	 * memory on background requests, panicking the kernel. The vendor
+	 * hook is therefore disabled for all FUSE requests. FUSE is not
+	 * block I/O, so no QoS tagging is lost.
+	 *
+	 * Disabled: trace_android_vh_queue_request_and_unlock(&fiq->waitq, sync);
+	 */
 	fiq->ops->wake_pending_and_unlock(fiq, sync);
 }
